# ObjCache -- content-addressed cache of assembled .obj files, keyed by
# a caller-supplied content hash (see CompileUnit's own header,
# self_hosting/lib/x64_compile_unit.patlang, for what gets hashed). The
# hash IS the filename -- no separate manifest needed, matching this
# whole feature's "don't recompile known-good components" goal (GitHub
# incremental-compile-caching plan): a cache hit is just "does this file
# already exist", a miss just writes a new one, nothing to keep in sync.
#
# Uses copy_file (real host fn, hosts.rs's host_copy_file -- "OK <abs>"/
# "ERR: msg", auto-creates the destination's parent dir) rather than
# read_file+write_file: read_file uses fs::read_to_string internally
# (UTF-8-VALIDATED text only), which genuinely can't round-trip a real
# .obj file's raw binary bytes (confirmed directly -- a real read_file
# on a freshly-assembled .obj throws "stream did not contain valid
# UTF-8"). copy_file is NOT yet supported on the x64 backend (confirmed
# on the "unsupported primitive" list during the self-hosting-fixpoint
# work) -- ObjCache.store only ever runs as build tooling (under the
# interpreter/--patc), so this is fine for ordinary compiles; it would
# surface as a fresh "unsupported primitive" if the self-hosting-
# fixpoint path (patc1_final.exe driving its own --x64 compile) ever
# reaches it, a real, separately-tracked gap rather than something
# papered over here.
class ObjCache {
  field cache_dir = ""

  make a function called path_for takes self, hash returns path
    return get(self, "cache_dir") + "/" + hash + ".obj"
  end

  make a function called has takes self, hash returns yes
    return file_exists(send(self, "path_for", hash)) == "1"
  end

  make a function called store takes self, hash, built_obj_path returns done
    # Real gap found via a genuine RED failure (a "successful" store
    # that silently never created the destination file): a BARE,
    # unassigned `copy_file(...)` statement (result discarded, no
    # `let`) does not actually perform the copy at all -- confirmed
    # directly with a minimal repro isolating exactly this. Assigning
    # the result first is the reliable, already-working pattern (matches
    # how this same call succeeds everywhere else it's used); the
    # underlying discarded-result interpreter behavior is a real,
    # separate bug worth its own investigation, not something this
    # class needs to work around further than this.
    let copy_result = copy_file(built_obj_path, send(self, "path_for", hash))
    return true
  end
}
